ana-cs: Heads-up: Aurora (mobile fintech) onboarding blocked. After a staged rollout ~280 Android + iOS clients cold-starting, they see a burst of 401s then 429s during SDK init.

jordan-platform: Can you paste a short log excerpt and SDK version? Are they fetching ephemeral tokens from their backend?

ana-cs: they use ephemeral session tokens from their backend /session. SDK version reported: android 1.3.7, ios 1.3.7. Log snippet from a repro device:

```
I/Networking: POST /v1/completions 401 Authorization: missing
I/Networking: fetching session token from /session
I/Networking: POST /v1/completions 429 rate_limit window=60s
```

Pattern: many simultaneous init calls from multiple app components, not awaiting token promise. They also have a short client retry that immediately retries on 401.

li-eng: Likely token stampede + retry amplification + pooled connection header reuse issue. Quick checklist: (1) ensure app awaits token before making generative calls; (2) serialize SDK.init path; (3) inject Authorization header per-request (Interceptor) rather than relying on pooled connections; (4) change retry-on-401 to refresh+single-replay with jitter/backoff.

sam-support: Customer-facing remediation we usually send: gate init behind a single entrypoint (AtomicBoolean/synchronized), set client-side preflight coalescing in SDK, reduce max preflight concurrency to 2-3, add a small randomized delay (200-1500ms) after token fetch before heavy fanout, and avoid immediate aggressive retries. Example Android snippet we provide:

```
// serialized init
private final AtomicBoolean initOnce = new AtomicBoolean(false);
if (initOnce.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
  executor.execute(() -> {
    String t = blockingFetchSession();
    Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(200, 1200)); // jitter
    RedwoodSdk.init(context, new SdkConfig.Builder()
      .maxPreflightConcurrency(3)
      .preflightCoalesce(true)
      .tokenRefreshJitterMs(800)
      .build(), t);
    initOnce.set(false);
  });
}
// OkHttp Interceptor ensures header per request
```

li-eng: On iOS same principle: single init entrypoint + per-request header injection (NSURLSessionDelegate/Request adapter) + small post-token jitter. Also recommend lowering connection pool size from default when devices spawn many short-lived threads.

jordan-platform: Short-term server-side smoothing: have their /session endpoint reuse a recently minted ephemeral token for a small window (2-4s) when you detect concurrent token requests, or gate token issuance behind a tiny semaphore. This prevents a thundering herd of unique tokens that still trigger auth checks and retries.

sam-support: Also advise upgrade: mobile SDK 1.4.2 contains a fix around token-refresh race and exposes preflight knobs. Recommend bump before next rollout.

metrics-bot: [alert] edge.rate.aurora.prod spike -> 429s +390% in 5m. Notifying @jordan-platform

ana-cs: I've sent the quick patch to Aurora: single-init guard, token jitter 800ms, maxPreflight=3, and changed retry to refresh+single-replay with exponential backoff.

li-eng: If they still see elevated 429s after that, we'll ask for an SDK debug bundle (thread dumps, connection pool config) and propose a brief screen share to inspect race timing. Consider suggesting HTTP/2 keepalive tweaks for worker threads.

jordan-platform: Monitoring dashboards show 429s dropped ~85% in the last 10m after their patch landed. Good outcome. Please add a short onboarding checklist item about "mobile init: single entrypoint + token jitter" in Confluence.

ana-cs: Confirmed with customer: rollout stable, no further auth/429 spikes. Closing ticket, will file onboarding note and paste KB snippet. Thanks team :thumbsup:
